Sculpt and rig the **Caldera Scald**, the field tower of the Holdfast — a low, round,
bulbous brass pressure drum ringed by a circle of short radial iron nozzle cowls pointing
outward in every direction, like a steam manifold, with no barrel at all — as a
{{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the
`voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting opaque cells** one operation at a time, and you
**author its idle and emit animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells you
*what the Scald is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you break the tower
into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every form are **entirely
yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a tower whose drum pumps and whose cowls open needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the ordered
operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the
rig you define are your submission.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each a parent
and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part
<name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is sculpted **separately** in its
own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there
is no transparency. Each call **records only** — it appends the operation to that part's log and
renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render` when you want to (re)draw the per-part previews
(`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled scene.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name drum
voxel-anim sphere --part drum --cx 18 --cy 16 --cz 18 --r 12 --color "#c8a24a"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining parts, setting
pivots, defining joints, and defining/keyframing animations), and `voxel-anim <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Run
`voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see that part's progress **and the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`,
`scene/top.png`)** — the whole Scald composed from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim
render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that the drum reads
round and low, the cowls ring it evenly on every side, and the ring runs unbroken. Because each
part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the
finished Scald; the scene simply composes them there.

