## What to deliver

Build and rig the Scald up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png` matches the
brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together correctly, and the rig is
complete: a low, round, bulbous brass pressure drum founded on the ground, a circle of short
radial iron nozzle cowls pointing outward in every direction with no barrel at all, and a
contiguous verdigris accent ring of those cowls sculpted **only** in `#2f7d72` and appearing
nowhere else on the model. **Author the two required animations** as F-curves with the
`voxel-anim` animation subcommands: an **`idle`** (the drum swelling and contracting as it holds
pressure and the cowls flexing, base fixed) and an **`emit`** (the cowls opening and the drum
pumping continuously to vent a sustained steam field — a continuous field, never discrete shots,
base fixed). The animations and the accent region are the fixed contract — produce all of them;
the parts and joints that realize them are yours. You may add your own extra parts, joints, or
animations on top, but do not drop or contradict the required `idle` and `emit`. Before you
finish, run `voxel-anim render` so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built
from — an unrendered part is scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one
part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant
of an animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs and
`rig.json` are your finished submission.
